  "id": "huju_shanghai_opera",
  "name": "Huju (Shanghai opera)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Shanghai-dialect opera that grew from the tanhuang folk-song and story-singing of the Shanghai countryside, known in turn as huaguxi, bentan and shenqu, and took the name Huju in 1941; through the twentieth century it specialised in modern-dress plays of city life and entered China's national intangible heritage list in 2006. Its lead fiddle is a bamboo-tube erhu (the erhu card stands in), joined by pipa, yangqin, sanxian and bamboo flute, with clappers for the beat.",
  "recipe": "classic early-1940s huju, art Music, east Asian branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. bamboo silk bamboo stick horsehair threaded between strings erhu, pipa, traditional soft attack bamboo yangqin, sanxian, bamboo credited bamboo flute unspecified, paiban. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
